    {
      "source": "Wu Blockchain (Telegram)",
      "type": "telegram",
      "headline": "Chinese Miner Jiang Zhuoer Exits DeFi Altcoins After Market Call Misses, Pauses Trading Jiang Zhuoer, founder of BTC.TOP and a prominent Chinese miner, said he has sold all of his DeFi altcoin holdings and moved to a hedged neutral position after Bitcoin’s latest decline invalidated parts of his earlier bullish thesis. He said BTC broke below support near $63,853 and $64,100 and approached his roughly $61,576 invalidation level, adding that a move below $57,800 in a late-bear-market scenario could bring sharper losses for smaller DeFi tokens. Jiang said he plans to step away from trading for a period before considering new positions. — link",
      "category": "Market & Macro",
      "importance": 1.095,
      "age_hours": 24.0,
      "source_quality": "social",
      "evidence_status": "unconfirmed-social",
      "asset_scope": "market-wide"
    },
    {
      "source": "Wu Blockchain (Telegram)",
      "type": "telegram",
      "headline": "64,000 Bitcoin Mining Machines Hijacked, Redirecting 13 EH/s to OCEAN Pool Bitcoin News reported that approximately 64,000 Bitcoin mining machines, representing about 13 EH/s of hashrate, were hijacked around 20:00 U.S. Central Time on November 14. The attackers redirected the hashrate to OCEAN and replaced the mining payout address with one they controlled, briefly pushing the pool's hashrate close to 50 EH/s. CoreX Hosting said the incident appeared to have ended on November 15, with the affected hashrate falling to around 100 PH/s. The attackers failed to mine any blocks and received no block rewards, although affected miners lost mining revenue during the incident. A potential compromise of Luxor Commander software has not been independently confirmed. — link",
      "category": "Market & Macro",
      "importance": 1.095,
      "age_hours": 24.0,
      "source_quality": "social",
      "evidence_status": "unconfirmed-social",
      "asset_scope": "market-wide"
    },
    {
      "source": "Wu Blockchain (Telegram)",
      "type": "telegram",
      "headline": "CZ: Binance Did Not Sell Crypto, I Bought Crypto Recently and Expect Continued Growth Over the Medium to Long Term Binance founder CZ said the exact reasons for the recent crypto market decline remain unclear, citing several potential factors: capital shifting toward Anthropic’s IPO; approximately $1.9 billion in net outflows from U.S. spot Bitcoin ETFs over seven trading sessions through November 13, alongside outflows from Ether ETFs; security concerns following the Binance hack, thefts from iPhone-linked crypto wallets, a Chrome exploit and a Microsoft outage; hotter-than-expected U.S. inflation fueling expectations of another Fed rate hike, with higher yields and a stronger dollar potentially weighing on risk assets; and concerns over potentially stricter U.S. crypto regulation following the midterm elections. CZ stressed that Binance had not sold crypto and said he had personally bought crypto in recent days after receiving some stablecoins. He added that he expects crypto to continue growing over the medium to long term. — link",
      "category": "Market & Macro",
      "importance": 1.095,
